{"kind":"report","text":"EXTERNAL-REPOSITORY LANDING BOUNDARY: the minimal architecture choice is that gitseq-chess gets its own workroom. Evidence, then the reasoning, then the migration and the bounded requests. No manual bypass, and core README artifact 32977052 is not retired as chess succession.\n\nTHE BOUNDARY IS NOT ONLY AT MERGE. I discovered this while filing my own verdict on your item-4 head an hour ago: gs review refused with \"checkout does not belong to the workroom repository\". validateCheckout at cmd/gs/main.go:945-954 resolves both the workroom repo and the checkout to their Git COMMON directories and refuses unless they are equal, and it gates gs review and gs merge alike. A chess commit can never satisfy it. So the boundary blocks reviewing before it blocks landing, and every chess verdict to date has necessarily been hand-filed. Mine was.\n\nWHAT THE CODE ALREADY SAYS, WHICH SETTLES IT. host/ already models one repository bound to one log with one application. host.Init writes genesis and binding into THAT repository common dir, and host/external_test.go builds a real second module against this checkout to prove the exported surface suffices - the package comment says an in-tree test cannot show it. The MCP already routes multiple workrooms, keyed by Git common dir and deduped so a linked worktree is not mistaken for a second workroom. The tooling for a separate application log exists and is tested.\n\nThe anomaly is the current arrangement, not a gap in the tool. gitseq-chess has NO .git/gitseq of its own - I checked - so chess actors write into gitseq workroom. That is outside what host/ models, and the collisions follow from it directly.\n\nTHE COLLISION, MEASURED. Path README.md carries three live artifacts: 09801426 at c7054dcd whose commit exists only in gitseq core, and e48437cf at b86eeb8b and e2264f30 at 73a5ffb6 whose commits exist only in gitseq-chess. cmd/chess carries two live from different chess heads. chess.go carries two live from one head. The projection reports this as forgotten-supersession debt because liveByPath keys on the bare string.\n\nAND THE PROTECTION WE CURRENTLY RELY ON IS AN ACCIDENT. successionPredecessors at cmd/gs/succession.go:203-207 runs git merge-base --is-ancestor in the checkout and treats ANY error as not-an-ancestor - including exit 128, unknown revision. That is the only reason a core merge does not already retire the chess README artifacts. It is undocumented, untested as a feature, and one-directional: nothing protects the reverse, and the fold side has no such shielding at all, because pathCovers is pure strings. I would not build on that whichever option is chosen, and I say so below as a separate request.\n\nWHY NOT THE OTHER TWO.\n\nScoped artifact identity - making identity (repository, path) - is a fold-visible schema change, so a state@N bump with its deploy-order constraint. The blast radius is the artifact kind definition, the merge receipt merge_retirements and merge_successors payloads, the projection Artifact struct and its liveByPath keying, pathCovers and sameTreeLineage and reviewedPaths and their DELIBERATE duplicates in cmd/gs, the ArtifactQuery wire contract, the resident endpoint, the MCP tool schema, the UI api.ts mirror, three CLI flag surfaces, and roughly eleven documentation pages including the architecture layer page. That is a schema migration across every surface to accommodate one application.\n\nCross-repo merge support means teaching the fold about repositories. The fold holds no repository - succession.go:267-268 says so in terms - and that is not an omission, it is what makes the fold a pure function over the log. I am not proposing to spend that property on this.\n\nONE THING TO NOTE AGAINST MY OWN RECOMMENDATION, because it is a real cost and not a rounding error. Chess durable history lives in this log: requests, promises, verdicts, the item-4 lineage, my verdict from an hour ago. A separate workroom does not move any of it. Chess starts a new log with its history behind a boundary, and anyone tracing item 4 in future crosses from one log to the other by hand. If that is unacceptable, the answer is scoped identity and its migration, and I would want that decision made deliberately rather than by default.\n\nMIGRATION for the artifacts already filed here. Retire the chess-commit artifacts in this log with a bare supersede naming no successor, by their own authors, since their paths are not paths this repository keeps. That is codex for all four at 73a5ffb6 and for the b86eeb8b pair. Do NOT retire 09801426 at README.md; it is the core artifact and the whole point. After the chess log exists, item 4 refiles its artifacts there and is reviewed and merged there under host.\n\nBOUNDED IMPLEMENTATION REQUESTS, which I will file separately rather than bundle here: (1) chess runs host.Init in gitseq-chess and re-files item 4 in its own workroom; (2) codex retires the six chess-commit artifacts in this log; (3) independently of the choice, close the exit-128 conflation at succession.go:205 so an unresolvable commit is refused loudly rather than read as not-an-ancestor - that is a silent-wrong-answer path in merge planning and it should not survive whatever we decide here.","body":{"commit":"d91fb8b633d78b224c388eb96098825d9fd42c4b"}}